{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "Baseline one-register-per-micro-rotation pipeline. It gives the highest Fmax and simplest control, but may use more FFs. Searching it ensures we do not miss the case where m=1 is area-optimal. The ranges focus on the accuracy-feasible, low-area region: data_width 12-16 and n_iter 8-16 should cross the 2^-10 error constraint, while small guards keep area low. Rounding is included because it can improve accuracy at little area cost."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "why": "Registers every m stages, so it can reduce FF count at the cost of lower Fmax. Since the throughput constraint is only 1 MSPS, this trade-off is likely favourable for the area objective. The extra m parameter creates a richer Pareto front, so it receives the larger budget share. m=1 is covered by the pipelined family. The same focused accuracy/area ranges are used, with m 2-8 to explore the FF-vs-Fmax trade-off."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) is easily satisfied by both pipelined families because they produce one result per cycle; even a modest Fmax of a few MHz exceeds 1 MSPS. Therefore the binding constraints are area (min LUTs+FFs) and accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-10). Accuracy depends only on data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, and rounding, so we focus the search on the region where accuracy crosses 10 bits (data_width ~12-16, n_iter ~8-16) and let the guards/rounding provide fine trade-offs. For area, pipelined_m can reduce flip-flops by registering every m stages, trading Fmax for area; since throughput is not tight, larger m may yield lower LUT+FF. We include the plain pipelined family as the m=1 baseline. Budget is split to give more evaluations to pipelined_m because it has an extra parameter and likely contains the area-optimal designs, while still covering pipelined to avoid missing a better m=1 solution. All 100 evaluations are allocated in this first round according to the shares above."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Constraints are demonstrably reachable, so `infeasible` is wrong: 29 of 60 evaluations were feasible (21 unique), throughput >= 1 MSPS is met with a 0% violation rate (best seen 282 MSPS), and max_abs_err <= 2^-10 is met with 48% of evaluations feasible and best seen 2^-12.86. The feasible Pareto front already covers the trade-off well against the spec's HV reference point: luts_plus_ffs spans 788..1141 against ref 1500, and accuracy_bits spans 10.6..12.9 against ref 10, giving HV 1381. The spec's selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) is served directly by the front's corner: pipelined_m [W=16 N=12 angle_guard=1 frac_guard=0 round m=8] at 788 LUT+FF, err 2^-10.56 (<= 2^-10) and 52.7 MSPS (>= 1 MSPS), with power_index 0.0296 and 145 FFs. This is the final round, so no further refinement, widening, family addition or full-range front mapping can be executed; the run ends with a feasible, constraint-satisfying front whose selection corner is already identified."
}
